content.thinking:
Interesting! The react-server build only exports `useCallback`, `useMemo`, `useId`, (and probably `use`), but it does NOT export `useRef`, `useState`, or `useContext`! So `React.useCallback` does work in RSC (that's why #3477 was able to use `useComposedRefs` → `useCallback` without immediately breaking RSC).

So then, why did #3477 cause an RSC error? It must be the `'use client'` directive that was added to index.ts. When you add `'use client'` to a package's entry, the entire package becomes a client reference in the RSC graph. In the ssr-testing app, `server.tsx` (a server component) imports `Slot` from 'radix-ui' — which resolves to the entry of `@radix-ui/react-slot` — and if that entry is `use client`, then the `Slot` component becomes a client reference, and the server component can only pass serializable props. `Slot.Slottable` as a client reference is... hmm, actually client references can be rendered from server components. But wait — the `radix-ui` umbrella package re-exports many things. The `use client` at the slot entry propagates through it.

Actually, the real breakage is this: when slot becomes `use client`, `Slottable` and `Slot` become client references. A server component renders `<Slot.Root>` — that's allowed (it becomes a client reference with serializable props). But in `server.tsx`, the `Slot` component is used as a value (`const Comp = asChild ? Slot.Root : 'a'` — that's fine)... hmm.

